# Line-ending and classification rules.
#
# Deliberately not a repository-wide policy: `* text=auto` would renormalize
# every existing file and produce a large diff unrelated to any change being
# made. These rules cover only the paths where the line ending is load-bearing.

# Vendored third-party sources. Each file's SHA-256 is recorded in
# CHECKSUMS.txt, so a checkout that rewrote them to CRLF would both fail that
# verification and compile different bytes than the ones reviewed and recorded.
# Kept diffable: reviewing what changes inside the runtime across an upgrade is
# a reason this code is vendored rather than referenced.
embedded/thirdparty/** eol=lf
embedded/thirdparty/** linguist-vendored

# Generated sources, once committed, are compared against freshly generated
# output by the CI freshness check. Generated text written on Windows would
# otherwise arrive as CRLF and produce diffs that have nothing to do with
# content. These rules must be in place before the first generated file is
# committed: attributes do not retroactively normalize what is already in
# history.
embedded/boards/*/generated/** eol=lf
embedded/proto-c/*/generated/** eol=lf

# nanopb output is machine-written and need not count towards language
# statistics. Board files are deliberately left unmarked: seeing what a ChibiOS
# bump changes in board.c is the reason for committing them at all.
embedded/proto-c/*/generated/*.pb.c linguist-generated
embedded/proto-c/*/generated/*.pb.h linguist-generated
